      "writtenOn": "2026-05-22T17:44:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The EC protocol transmits its headers in little-endian. While the packing logic correctly uses `std::byteswap` to handle this when sending data, the receiving logic reads the multi-byte `result` and `data_length` fields natively. If this tool is executed on a big-endian architecture, it will misinterpret these fields (e.g., a length of `0x0001` would be read as `0x0100`), leading to false error detections and out-of-bounds memory access.\n\n### Detailed Fix\n\n**Location:** `util/egis/ec_comm.cc` inside `EcComm::SendCommand()`\n\nExtract the fields into local variables using the endianness helper (defined in Fix 3 below) immediately after checksum validation.\n\n```c\n  EcHostCommandResponse *resp_hdr \u003d\n      reinterpret_cast\u003cEcHostCommandResponse *\u003e(resp_buf.data());\n      \n  // Fix: Convert little-endian fields to native host endianness\n  uint16_t result \u003d FromLittleEndian(resp_hdr-\u003eresult);\n  uint16_t data_length \u003d FromLittleEndian(resp_hdr-\u003edata_length);\n\n  if (result !\u003d 0) {\n    std::println(stderr, \"[ERROR] EC device returned error code: {}\", result);\n    return std::unexpected(EcError::kDeviceError);\n  }\n\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e resp_out;\n  if (data_length \u003e 0) {\n    size_t header_len \u003d sizeof(EcHostCommandResponse);\n    if (header_len + data_length \u003e resp_buf.size()) {\n      std::println(\n          stderr, \"[ERROR] EC response data truncated. Claimed: {}, Actual: {}\",\n          data_length, resp_buf.size() - header_len);\n      return std::unexpected(EcError::kResponseTooShort);\n    }\n    resp_out.assign(resp_buf.begin() + header_len,\n                    resp_buf.begin() + header_len + data_length);\n  }\n\n```",
